WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.520
Brady and Neil thought it might be fun to try and add a piece of metallic Lithium to 7 Up

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:13.900
The reason for this is that long ago 7 Up used to contain Lithium salts

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.800
So they thought it'd be a bit of a joke

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.340
They bought a bottle of 7 Up... this is not product placement

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.960
and they poured some into a beaker and dropped in a piece of Lithium

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.760
Of course it bubbled - partly the CO2 coming out of the fizzy drink, partly the hydrogen from the reaction

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:59.480
But what was surprising was that the solution started to go pale green and then it went to reddish brown colour

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.940
and by the time I went to the lab, (it) looked like beer

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:11.260
The obvious first stage is Lithium metal reacts with water to form Lithium hydroxide which is an alkali

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:18.160
That alkali might react further with the other components of 7 Up

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:26.820
And if you read about it, it contains sugar, natural lemon and lime flavor, whatever that is,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.180
and it also contains "no caffeine" so there's no caffeine chemistry here

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:38.920
The first question is how would the acidity or alkalinity of the solution change

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.440
So we measured the pH of fresh 7 Up

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:50.000
which turned out to be slightly acidic, as you might expect, if you dissolved carbon dioxide into water

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.520
And we measured the acidity of the 7 Up plus Lithium

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.700
and again as expected it was pretty alkaline

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:06.360
So a possible explanation for the colour is that it was produced by the alkaline conditions

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.980
and the alkali was causing a reaction of the other components of the 7 Up

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.940
Lithium salts are completely colourless. They're all white just like table salt

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.020
So the question is why is it going coloured and brown

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.080
I suggested an experiment

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:35.120
If it was the alkali, we should get a similar effect if we put Sodium Hydroxide, which is a strong alkali, into some 7 Up

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.320
To begin with nothing happened

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.920
Neil and Brady got quite excited

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.600
"The Professor's wrong!"

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.440
You see, one up for them

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:56.580
But then as we watched, the pellets of Sodium Hydroxide started developing a red colour

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.200
almost as if they were bleeding

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.220
It was really rather beautiful

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.440
and the colour began to develop

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:10.060
It was reacting slowly - much more slowly than the reaction that we saw with Lithium

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:17.700
We noticed that the Lithium hydroxide one - the one that we put the lithium metal in - the solution was quite hot

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:24.900
And Neil dipped a thermocouple into the Sodium Hydroxide plus 7 Up

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.500
and found that the surface was nearly at room temperature

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.480
but down where the colour was developing, it was quite warm - nearly 50 degrees

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.540
You might say, "Why wasn't it convecting?"

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:44.540
Well of course the concentrated solution of Sodium Hydroxide is very dense

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.960
so you get less convection than you would normally in water

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.020
We added some more Sodium Hydroxide and the colour developed

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.860
And so, the reason why temperature is important is because

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:09.260
most reactions of organic compounds, like the sugars in the 7 Up, double their rate for each 10 degrees centigrade that you go up

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:19.920
So if you go from room temperature, 20 to 30 to 40 to 50, the rate will go up about 8 times - doubling each time

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.280
So that would explain the difference

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.180
By this time, the Lithium beaker was looking pretty dark

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.060
and you couldn't really tell what colour it was

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.960
So I suggested that we dilute it a bit into water

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:42.540
So we got a beaker of water, not 7 Up, just pure water and added a little bit of the solution

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.560
and it looked as of yellowy brown colour

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.600
Suddenly I thought perhaps whatever coloured compound we'd made might be an indicator

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.840
you know that changes colour when we go from acid to alkaline

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.000
So Neil had some concentrated Sulfuric acid which is a bit fierce

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:11.200
so he diluted that and we poured in the Sulfuric acid and much to my pleasure, it went completely colourless

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:17.380
So whatever we made changed colour depending on the pH, depending on the acidity

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.280
But then, I wondered what would happen if we added more alkali

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.200
And when we added more alkali

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.160
it changed back to colour

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.640
Now of course there are 2 explanations

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:38.900
The first one is we may have created an indicator out of 7 Up

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.760
The other explanation is that

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:48.680
whatever was reacting in the 7 Up, there was still some in the water and that reacted with the alkali again

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:57.120
So you can't be absolutely sure what happended in the final stage but it seemed to behave like an indicator

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.580
When we put in more alkali, it could be that that compound changed back

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:07.780
or, it could be that there was still sugar in the solution and we just made more of the starting compound

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:12.100
And you can't really tell the difference without more careful experiments

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:23.260
[Coke in Liquid Nitrogen] Looked like an artwork and I persuaded Brady and Neil that we should watch it melting

